/* chatwire.rc — the DLL, carried inside the executable.

   chatwire.exe is meant to be one file you can hand to somebody: no folder, no
   "keep these two together", no way to end up with an injector and a DLL from
   different builds.  A Windows resource is how a PE image carries a payload,
   and it costs nothing at build time -- the linker copies the bytes in, which
   is not true of the usual alternative (generating a C array), where a 3 MB DLL
   becomes a 20 MB source file the compiler then has to chew through.

   RCDATA is the raw-bytes resource type: no parsing, no interpretation, exactly
   what was on disk.  The path is substituted by CMake at configure time because
   the DLL lives in the build tree, which the source tree cannot name.

   Backslashes in the path are doubled by CMake before substitution: an .rc
   string literal escapes them the way C does, so a raw Windows path would turn
   \t into a tab and fail to open a file with a very confusing name. */
CHATWIRE_DLL RCDATA "@CHATWIRE_DLL_RC_PATH@"
